+# Blocked: the slug test suite contains contradictory requirements
+
+`test_slug.py` (which must not be changed) cannot pass as a whole, because two
+of its tests assert different results for the identical input:
+
+- `test_preserves_underscores` (Spec 4.2) expects
+  `slugify("user_id field") == "user_id-field"`.
+- `test_underscores_become_dashes` (Spec 4.5) expects
+  `slugify("user_id field") == "user-id-field"`.
+
+A single deterministic function cannot return both values for one input, so at
+most one of these two tests can pass. No implementation can satisfy the suite
+without a test-awareness hack (for example inspecting the caller), which would
+be a workaround rather than a fix.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+`slug.py` was implemented to satisfy every non-contradictory test: lowercase,
+punctuation removed, dashes collapsed, accents folded to ASCII, and leading /
+trailing separators trimmed.
+
+Because Spec 4.5 requires slugs to contain only `[a-z0-9-]`, underscores are
+treated as separators and become dashes. That choice makes
+`test_underscores_become_dashes` pass and leaves only
+`test_preserves_underscores` failing.
+
+Current result: `Ran 7 tests`, `FAILED (failures=1)`.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+The content team must decide which rule wins for underscores — Spec 4.2 or
+Spec 4.5 — and remove or correct the losing test. The implementation can then
+be adjusted in one line (`slug.py`, the `re.sub` character class).
